# GEMINI_API_KEY: Required for Gemini AI API calls.
# AI Studio automatically injects this at runtime from user secrets.
# Users configure this via the Secrets panel in the AI Studio UI.
GEMINI_API_KEY="MY_GEMINI_API_KEY"

# APP_URL: The URL where this applet is hosted.
# AI Studio automatically injects this at runtime with the Cloud Run service URL.
# Used for self-referential links, OAuth callbacks, and API endpoints.
APP_URL="MY_APP_URL"

# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY: Required for Claude AI fallback.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# OPENAI_API_KEY: Required for ChatGPT AI fallback.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# --- MCP server (api/mcp.ts) — read + write pool data for MCP clients (e.g. ChatGPT, Claude) ---

# MCP_BEARER_TOKEN: Required to enable the endpoint. Shared secret clients
# must send as `Authorization: Bearer <token>`. Without it, /api/mcp
# returns 503. Generate one with e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32`. Grants full
# access: both the read tools and the write tools (add/complete tasks,
# adjust inventory, log a photo-backed reading).
MCP_BEARER_TOKEN=

# MCP_READONLY_BEARER_TOKEN: Optional. A second, separately-revocable
# bearer token that only grants the read tools — the four write tools
# aren't just rejected for it, they're never registered, so a client using
# this token doesn't even see them in tools/list. Hand this one to a
# less-trusted connector (e.g. a third-party client you don't want able to
# log readings or touch inventory) instead of the full MCP_BEARER_TOKEN.
# Generate it the same way, with a different value.
MCP_READONLY_BEARER_TOKEN=

# FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT: Required. A Firebase service-account key (Project
# Settings -> Service Accounts -> Generate new private key), as either raw
# JSON or base64-encoded JSON (base64 is usually easier to paste into a
# single-line env var field). Used by firebase-admin to read/write
# Firestore and Cloud Storage server-side, bypassing firestore.rules — see
# POOLSTATUS_OWNER_UID below. The storage bucket itself is read from the
# committed firebase-applet-config.json, not an env var — poolstatus_log_reading
# uses it to store a reading's evidence photo.
FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=

# POOLSTATUS_OWNER_UID: Required (or POOLSTATUS_OWNER_EMAIL below). The
# Firebase Auth uid whose pool data the MCP server exposes — every query is
# hard-pinned to this uid server-side. Find it in Firebase Console ->
# Authentication -> Users.
POOLSTATUS_OWNER_UID=

# POOLSTATUS_OWNER_EMAIL: Alternative to POOLSTATUS_OWNER_UID — the account's
# sign-in email, looked up via Firebase Auth on first use. Prefer
# POOLSTATUS_OWNER_UID (no extra lookup); set this only if the uid isn't
# handy. Ignored if POOLSTATUS_OWNER_UID is set.
POOLSTATUS_OWNER_EMAIL=

# --- Pool controller sync (api/cron/sync-pool-controller.ts) — pulls telemetry
# --- from a Hanna Cloud-connected controller (e.g. BL122) into `readings`.
# --- Also uses FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT and POOLSTATUS_OWNER_UID/EMAIL above.
# --- See CLAUDE.md's "Pool controller telemetry" section for the full picture.

# CRON_SECRET: Required to enable the endpoint. Shared secret that both
# Vercel Cron (set automatically as a header on its own invocations once
# this env var exists) and the GitHub Actions workflow authenticate with
# as `Authorization: Bearer <secret>`. Generate one with `openssl rand -hex 32`
# and also add it as a GitHub Actions repo secret of the same name.
CRON_SECRET=

# HANNA_CLOUD_EMAIL / HANNA_CLOUD_PASSWORD: Required. Credentials for the
# HannaCloud account (hannacloud.com) the BL122/BL132 controller is
# registered to — the same login used in Hanna's own web app. These are
# real account credentials, not an API key: never expose them to the
# client bundle, only read them server-side.
HANNA_CLOUD_EMAIL=
HANNA_CLOUD_PASSWORD=

# HANNA_CLOUD_DEVICE_ID: Optional. The controller's device ID (DID), only
# needed if the HannaCloud account has more than one BL12x/BL13x device —
# with exactly one, it's auto-detected.
HANNA_CLOUD_DEVICE_ID=

# HANNA_CLOUD_DEVICE_TIMEZONE / HANNA_CLOUD_DEVICE_UTC_OFFSET_MINUTES:
# Optional, and set at most one. What the controller's OWN clock is set to.
#
# The controller sends a bare wall-clock time with no offset, and both Hanna's
# decoder and this app read that as UTC by default. If the keypad clock was
# instead set to local time — the natural thing to do, since that's what the
# device displays to whoever is standing in front of it — every reading
# arrives labelled hours in the future and the clock-skew guard rejects it
# (diagnostic HC_TIMESTAMP_FUTURE, whose log line gives the exact offset in
# aheadSeconds: a whole number of hours means this is what you want).
#
# This is NOT a way to loosen that guard. The guard catches a clock that is
# wrong; these say the clock is right and name the zone it keeps, so the
# stored instant is correct rather than merely tolerated.
#
# Pick by whether the device itself observes daylight saving:
#   TIMEZONE           a canonical IANA zone (Europe/London), converted per
#                      reading, for a device that follows local clock changes.
#   UTC_OFFSET_MINUTES a fixed offset (60, or -330), for a device left on one
#                      offset all year — which a controller with no DST
#                      setting effectively is, even where DST is observed.
# Choosing wrongly is silent until a DST boundary, so prefer the fixed offset
# unless the device has been seen to change itself across one. A bad value
# fails the sync loudly rather than reverting to UTC.
HANNA_CLOUD_DEVICE_TIMEZONE=
HANNA_CLOUD_DEVICE_UTC_OFFSET_MINUTES=
